<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Adapted from FOPP "Naming Variables in For Loops" and "Keeping Track of
     Your Iterator Variable and Your Iterable". GNU FDL 1.3; see for-loops/ch-loops.ptx. -->
<section xml:id="sec-wp-naming">
  <title>&#x1F469;&#x200D;&#x1F4BB; Naming Loop Variables</title>
  <introduction>

    <p>
      The names you choose do not matter to the computer, but they matter a lot to you
      and to anyone who reads your code.
      Loops have a few naming habits that make them much easier to read.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Use a plural noun for an array</term>:
          <c>songs</c>, <c>scores</c>, <c>players</c>, <c>words</c>.
          The name tells you it holds many things.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>In a counting <c>for</c> loop, use <c>i</c></term> for the loop variable.
          Every programmer reads <c>i</c> as <q>the index.</q>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>When you save the current element in a variable, use the singular of the array name</term>:
          <c>let song = songs[i];</c>, <c>let score = scores[i];</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Compare these two loops. They do the same thing.
    </p>

    <program language="javascript">
      <code>
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; x.length; i++) {
    let y = x[i];
    console.log(y);
  }

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; genres.length; i++) {
    let genre = genres[i];
    console.log(genre);
  }
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      Without seeing how <c>x</c> was made, you have no idea what the first loop prints.
      The second loop tells you right away.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-keep-straight">
    <title>Keep the Array and the Loop Variable Straight</title>

    <p>
      A loop always has two different things in it, and mixing them up is a common mistake:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><p>The <term>array</term> is the whole collection. It does not change while the loop runs.</p></li>
      <li><p>The <term>loop variable</term> changes on every pass. In a counting loop, it is a number: the index.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      In a counting loop, the current element is <c>songs[i]</c>: the array name, then the loop variable in brackets.
      Writing <c>songs</c> alone means the whole array. Writing <c>i</c> alone means the index, not the song.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-wp-naming-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-naming-type" label="mc-naming-type">
      <statement>
        <p>In this loop, what data type is <c>i</c>?</p>
        <program language="javascript">
          <code>
  let songs = ["Hey Jude", "Imagine", "Respect"];

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; songs.length; i++) {
    console.log(songs[i]);
  }
          </code>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Number</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. <c>i</c> is an index: 0, then 1, then 2.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>String</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. <c>songs[i]</c> is a string, but <c>i</c> is the index.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Array</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. <c>songs</c> is the array. <c>i</c> is something else.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-naming-best" label="mc-naming-best">
      <statement>
        <p>An array holds the names of every city a band will visit on tour. Inside a loop over that array, which line is the best way to save the current element?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>let city = cities[i];</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. Plural for the array, singular for one element.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>let cities = city[i];</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. This works, but the names are backwards: the array holds many cities.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>let x = list[i];</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. This works, but the names do not say what the array holds.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
